<div class="bg-white">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-7xl divide-y divide-gray-900/10 px-6 py-24 sm:py-32 lg:px-8 lg:py-40">
    <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold leading-10 tracking-tight text-gray-900">
      Frequently asked questions
    </h1>
    <dl class="mt-10 space-y-8 divide-y divide-gray-900/10">
      <div id="what-is-youcongress" class="pt-8 lg:grid lg:grid-cols-12 lg:gap-8">
        <dt class="text-base font-semibold leading-7 text-gray-900 lg:col-span-5">
          What is YouCongress?
        </dt>
        <dd class="mt-4 lg:col-span-7 lg:mt-0">
          <p class="text-base leading-7 text-gray-600">
            YouCongress is a platform for improving democracy, starting with AI safety, governance, and the impact of AI on jobs. AI agents gather sourced statements, helping people explore different views, vote, and delegate to those they trust. Creators gain insights into their audiences. Our aim is to improve public debate, help decision-makers understand the evidence and the people affected by their choices, and help new trusted voices emerge, including people who have never considered running for office.
          </p>
          <p class="text-base leading-7 text-gray-600 pt-4">
            YouCongress is a non-profit, <.link
              class="underline"
              target="_blank"
              href="https://github.com/youcongress/youcongress"
            >open-source</.link>&nbsp;project. We preserve the provenance of sourced quotes and claims and offer decision support rather than binding referendums.
          </p>
          <p class="text-base leading-7 text-gray-600 pt-4">
            Forecasters ask "What will happen?" We ask "What should we do?" and "What would we love to happen even when unlikely?" Inspired by Scott Alexander's idea of "Moloch" (misaligned incentives pushing everyone toward outcomes nobody wants), YouCongress makes shared preferences visible. It combines direct voting, optional delegation, and sourced expertise to help leaders understand public and expert values.
          </p>
          <p class="text-base leading-7 text-gray-600 pt-4">
            More about this on our <.link
              href="https://www.lesswrong.com/posts/4KjiZeAWc7Yv9oyCb/tackling-moloch-how-youcongress-offers-a-novel-coordination"
              target="_blank"
              class="underline"
            >LessWrong post</.link>.
          </p>
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div id="why-ai" class="pt-8 lg:grid lg:grid-cols-12 lg:gap-8">
        <dt class="text-base font-semibold leading-7 text-gray-900 lg:col-span-5">
          Why start with AI?
        </dt>
        <dd class="mt-4 lg:col-span-7 lg:mt-0">
          <p class="text-base leading-7 text-gray-600">
            AI raises connected questions about safety, governance, jobs, and who benefits from technological change. These questions bring together technical evidence, different values, and choices that affect many people. They give us a concrete place to test whether sourced statements and public participation can improve debate and inform decisions. Our broader mission is to improve democracy, and the approach can also be tested on other public questions.
          </p>
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div id="decision-makers" class="pt-8 lg:grid lg:grid-cols-12 lg:gap-8">
        <dt class="text-base font-semibold leading-7 text-gray-900 lg:col-span-5">
          How can YouCongress help decision-makers?
        </dt>
        <dd class="mt-4 lg:col-span-7 lg:mt-0">
          <p class="text-base leading-7 text-gray-600">
            Decision-makers can compare sourced positions on a proposal, examine objections, and explore participants’ views through opinions, votes, and delegation. Our aim is to help them understand both the evidence and the people affected by their choices, whether they are preparing a briefing, consulting on a policy, or weighing a decision.
          </p>
          <p class="text-base leading-7 text-gray-600 pt-4">
            Participants are self-selected, so results are not a representative poll. Decision-makers still need to assess the evidence, seek missing perspectives, and take responsibility for their choices. Our
            <.link href={~p"/theory-of-change"} class="underline">theory of change</.link>
            explains how we want to test this contribution.
          </p>
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div id="creators" class="pt-8 lg:grid lg:grid-cols-12 lg:gap-8">
        <dt class="text-base font-semibold leading-7 text-gray-900 lg:col-span-5">
          How can creators understand their audiences?
        </dt>
        <dd class="mt-4 lg:col-span-7 lg:mt-0">
          <p class="text-base leading-7 text-gray-600">
            With <.link href={~p"/reconsider"} class="underline">Reconsider</.link>, creators can pair an article or video with up to three YouCongress statements. Readers or viewers report their positions before and after the content in one form and can choose featured people as delegates. The shared results show reported changes in views and delegate selections, helping creators explore where participants agree, disagree, or reconsider.
          </p>
          <p class="text-base leading-7 text-gray-600 pt-4">
            Reconsider is in beta. The responses are self-reported and come from people who choose to participate; they do not represent the whole audience or prove that the content caused a change in opinion.
          </p>
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div id="trusted-voices" class="pt-8 lg:grid lg:grid-cols-12 lg:gap-8">
        <dt class="text-base font-semibold leading-7 text-gray-900 lg:col-span-5">
          How could new trusted voices emerge?
        </dt>
        <dd class="mt-4 lg:col-span-7 lg:mt-0">
          <p class="text-base leading-7 text-gray-600">
            People can share their reasoning, engage with objections, and earn trust from others who choose them as delegates. We hope this helps thoughtful contributors become known beyond their existing circles, including people who have never considered running for office. Some may discover an interest in public service; others may contribute through research, explanation, or community leadership.
          </p>
          <p class="text-base leading-7 text-gray-600 pt-4">
            This is an aim we want to test. Gaining delegates can indicate trust, but does not by itself demonstrate good judgment or readiness for office.
          </p>
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div id="epistemic-investigations" class="pt-8 lg:grid lg:grid-cols-12 lg:gap-8">
        <dt class="text-base font-semibold leading-7 text-gray-900 lg:col-span-5">
          How does YouCongress support epistemic investigations?
        </dt>
        <dd class="mt-4 lg:col-span-7 lg:mt-0">
          <p class="text-base leading-7 text-gray-600">
            YouCongress is designed to make investigations reusable instead of trapped in one-off reports or chat transcripts. It separates ingestion, structure, and assessment work so humans and AI systems can inspect and extend the same public record.
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-5 text-base leading-7 text-gray-600 pt-4">
            <li>
              <strong>Ingestion:</strong>
              capture sourced quotes, authors, dates, URLs, topics and verification status.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Structure:</strong>
              connect sources to claims, policies, vote inferences, halls and author pages.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Assessment:</strong>
              use the structured record to compare positions, reveal disagreement, and identify missing perspectives. This is the layer we most want to strengthen next.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div id="what-can-i-do" class="pt-8 lg:grid lg:grid-cols-12 lg:gap-8">
        <dt class="text-base font-semibold leading-7 text-gray-900 lg:col-span-5">
          What can users do?
        </dt>
        <dd class="mt-4 lg:col-span-7 lg:mt-0">
          <p class="text-base leading-7 text-gray-600">
            YouCongress is still in its infancy, but you already can:
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-5 text-base leading-7 text-gray-600">
            <li>explore verifiable quotes from experts and public figures</li>
            <li>
              create a <.link href={~p"/reconsider"} class="underline">Reconsider</.link>
              page to explore your audience’s views around an article or video.
            </li>
            <li>
              vote and share your perspective (votes are private unless you publish a comment).
            </li>
            <li>
              build a <.link href={~p"/council"} class="underline">Council</.link>
              of people you trust, with different lists for different topics. Your Council can determine your vote when you have not voted directly.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div id="delegation" class="pt-8 lg:grid lg:grid-cols-12 lg:gap-8">
        <dt class="text-base font-semibold leading-7 text-gray-900 lg:col-span-5">
          What is My Council?
        </dt>
        <dd class="mt-4 lg:col-span-7 lg:mt-0">
          <p class="text-base leading-7 text-gray-600">
            Your Council is your private choice of whose judgment to follow on YouCongress. You choose lists of people and the topics they apply to. On statements you have not voted on directly, their positions can determine a vote for you.
          </p>
          <p class="text-base leading-7 text-gray-600 pt-4">
            Start at <.link href={~p"/council"} class="underline">My Council</.link>. After signing up, choose people you trust for your All statements list and optionally say why you chose each person. Changes save automatically. You can then go to My Council to adjust your choices; publishing a list and inviting others are optional.
          </p>
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div id="council-topics" class="pt-8 lg:grid lg:grid-cols-12 lg:gap-8">
        <dt class="text-base font-semibold leading-7 text-gray-900 lg:col-span-5">
          Can I choose different people for different topics?
        </dt>
        <dd class="mt-4 lg:col-span-7 lg:mt-0">
          <p class="text-base leading-7 text-gray-600">
            Yes. Each Council entry pairs a list of people with a hall, which is a topic tag on statements. The default order is AI governance, Impact on labor, and All statements. Your first picks go into All statements; the topic lists start empty, so you can fill them in later.
          </p>
          <p class="text-base leading-7 text-gray-600 pt-4">
            You can add halls, reorder entries, and use the same hall more than once with different lists. All statements applies to every statement. You can add an All statements entry higher in the order for a small group you especially trust; the final All statements entry stays last as a fallback.
          </p>
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div id="council-votes" class="pt-8 lg:grid lg:grid-cols-12 lg:gap-8">
        <dt class="text-base font-semibold leading-7 text-gray-900 lg:col-span-5">
          How does my Council choose my vote?
        </dt>
        <dd class="mt-4 lg:col-span-7 lg:mt-0">
          <ol class="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-2 text-base leading-7 text-gray-600">
            <li>
              We check your entries from top to bottom, skipping halls that do not match the statement’s tags. All statements entries always match.
            </li>
            <li>
              Within each matching list, we compare For and Against. More For means For; more Against means Against. Abstentions and people without an eligible position do not count toward either side.
            </li>
            <li>
              A tie, an empty list, or an unavailable list moves us to the next matching entry. The first list that decides supplies your Council vote. If none decides, no Council vote is cast.
            </li>
          </ol>
          <p class="text-base leading-7 text-gray-600 pt-4">
            For example, 8 For and 0 Against gives a For vote, even if 1 person abstains and 49 have no position. A statement tagged only environment skips AI governance and Impact on labor, then uses All statements unless you have added an earlier matching entry that decides.
          </p>
          <p class="text-base leading-7 text-gray-600 pt-4">
            Your direct vote always takes priority, including a direct Abstain. Clearing your direct vote lets your Council apply again. On a statement, click “See how your Council voted” to see which lists were checked or skipped and who was For, Against, or Abstain, or had no eligible position.
          </p>
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div id="council-positions" class="pt-8 lg:grid lg:grid-cols-12 lg:gap-8">
        <dt class="text-base font-semibold leading-7 text-gray-900 lg:col-span-5">
          Which positions count toward my Council vote?
        </dt>
        <dd class="mt-4 lg:col-span-7 lg:mt-0">
          <p class="text-base leading-7 text-gray-600">
            A person’s own direct vote counts. A position inferred from a sourced quote counts when the quote, its relevance to the statement, and the inferred vote have positive verification: Endorsed, Verified, or AI Verified. Unverified, disputed, or unverifiable positions do not count.
          </p>
          <p class="text-base leading-7 text-gray-600 pt-4">
            We use each person’s own position, not votes they receive through their Council. You can inspect the people and positions behind your Council’s decision on the statement page and cast your own vote at any time.
          </p>
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div id="council-lists" class="pt-8 lg:grid lg:grid-cols-12 lg:gap-8">
        <dt class="text-base font-semibold leading-7 text-gray-900 lg:col-span-5">
          Can I use my own lists or someone else’s public list?
        </dt>
        <dd class="mt-4 lg:col-span-7 lg:mt-0">
          <p class="text-base leading-7 text-gray-600">
            Each Council entry uses one list: a private or public list you own, or a public list by someone else. “Choose people” or “Edit people” opens the people editor. To change the source list or hall, open the entry’s options and choose “Edit entry.”
          </p>
          <p class="text-base leading-7 text-gray-600 pt-4">
            Changes to a list you own apply everywhere you use that list. When you use someone else’s public list, your Council follows their membership updates. You cannot edit their list, but you can make a private copy and change it independently. Replacing or removing a Council entry does not delete the underlying list.
          </p>
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div id="council-privacy" class="pt-8 lg:grid lg:grid-cols-12 lg:gap-8">
        <dt class="text-base font-semibold leading-7 text-gray-900 lg:col-span-5">
          Is my Council public? Do I have to publish a list?
        </dt>
        <dd class="mt-4 lg:col-span-7 lg:mt-0">
          <p class="text-base leading-7 text-gray-600">
            Your Council assignments and their order are private. A public list shows its name, people, and reasons, but does not reveal whether you use it in your Council. Other list owners cannot see whether you use their lists either. Private lists and their reasons are visible only to you.
          </p>
          <p class="text-base leading-7 text-gray-600 pt-4">
            Publishing is optional. You can create named public lists to share people you value without using those lists to determine your votes. You can also invite others to choose their own Council without publishing yours.
          </p>
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div id="sourced-quotes" class="pt-8 lg:grid lg:grid-cols-12 lg:gap-8">
        <dt class="text-base font-semibold leading-7 text-gray-900 lg:col-span-5">
          How do sourced quotes work?
        </dt>
        <dd class="mt-4 lg:col-span-7 lg:mt-0">
          <p class="text-base leading-7 text-gray-600">
            Signed-in users can add real quotes from notable figures and experts, then link them to the claims and policy proposals they bear on.
          </p>
          <p class="text-base leading-7 text-gray-600 pt-4">
            Every sourced quote must include a source URL or, for books, PDFs, and paywalled sources, a sufficiently identifying source passage. This lets readers check the attribution and context.
          </p>
          <p class="text-base leading-7 text-gray-600 pt-4">
            These sourced quotes help inform voters and investigators by providing expert perspectives, documented positions, and reusable provenance on various topics.
          </p>
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div id="verify-quotes" class="pt-8 lg:grid lg:grid-cols-12 lg:gap-8">
        <dt class="text-base font-semibold leading-7 text-gray-900 lg:col-span-5">
          How are quotes verified? How can I help verify?
        </dt>
        <dd class="mt-4 lg:col-span-7 lg:mt-0">
          <p class="text-base leading-7 text-gray-600">
            Sourced quotes on YouCongress can be checked across three layers: whether the quote is authentic, whether it is relevant to the policy or claim where it appears, and whether the vote inferred from it is correct. Each check has its own status; on statement pages, one badge summarizes them together.
          </p>
          <p class="text-base leading-7 text-gray-600 pt-4">
            An individual verification can be:
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-5 text-base leading-7 text-gray-600">
            <li>
              <strong>Endorsed</strong>: the author personally confirmed the quote, relation, or vote, for example by signing in or by contacting an admin.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Verified</strong>: a human reviewer checked the source and marked the item as correct.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>AI Verified</strong>: the automated verifier checked the source and found support for the item.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Disputed</strong>: a reviewer or automated check found a problem, such as a mismatched quote, statement relation, or vote answer.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Unverifiable</strong>: the available source is not enough to confirm or reject the item.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>AI Unverifiable</strong>: the automated verifier could not confirm the item, usually because the source was inaccessible or inconclusive.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Unverified</strong>: no current positive, disputed, or unverifiable verification is recorded yet, or a reviewer cleared the status.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p class="text-base leading-7 text-gray-600 pt-4">
            On a statement page, the combined badge displays an AI Unverifiable check as Unverifiable.
          </p>
          <p class="text-base leading-7 text-gray-600 pt-4">
            A quote can still appear while some checks are pending. Downstream checks, such as relation and vote-answer verification, depend on the quote itself first being confirmed by a positive status like Endorsed, Verified, or AI Verified.
          </p>
          <p class="text-base leading-7 text-gray-600 pt-4">
            Want to help verify quotes? Email us at
            <.link href="mailto:verify@youcongress.org" class="underline" target="_blank">
              verify@youcongress.org
            </.link>
            and we will get you set up.
          </p>
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div id="my-profile" class="pt-8 lg:grid lg:grid-cols-12 lg:gap-8">
        <dt class="text-base font-semibold leading-7 text-gray-900 lg:col-span-5">
          How can I change/delete content attributed to me?
        </dt>
        <dd class="mt-4 lg:col-span-7 lg:mt-0">
          <p class="text-base leading-7 text-gray-600">
            If you find any quotes or content incorrectly attributed to you, or you want to change/delete content, please
            <%= if YouCongress.FeatureFlags.enabled?(:log_in_with_x) do %>
              <.link href="/auth/x" class="underline">sign in with X</.link>
              so you can do it directly or
            <% end %>
            contact us at
            <.link href="mailto:support@youcongress.org" class="underline">
              support@youcongress.org
            </.link>
            and we'll address it promptly.
          </p>
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div id="who-is-behind" class="pt-8 lg:grid lg:grid-cols-12 lg:gap-8">
        <dt class="text-base font-semibold leading-7 text-gray-900 lg:col-span-5">
          Who is behind YouCongress?
        </dt>
        <dd class="mt-4 lg:col-span-7 lg:mt-0">
          <p class="text-base leading-7 text-gray-600">
            YouCongress was started by <.link
              href="https://hecperez.com/"
              target="_blank"
              class="underline"
            >Hector Perez</.link>, with UX and design contributions from <.link
              href="https://www.gonzalocardenete.com/"
              target="_blank"
              class="underline"
            >Gonzalo Cardenete</.link>.
          </p>
          <p class="text-base leading-7 text-gray-600 pt-2">
            Read about their backgrounds on our <.link
              href={~p"/about#about-people"}
              class="underline"
            >About page</.link>.
          </p>
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div id="funding" class="pt-8 lg:grid lg:grid-cols-12 lg:gap-8">
        <dt class="text-base font-semibold leading-7 text-gray-900 lg:col-span-5">
          Who funds YouCongress?
        </dt>
        <dd class="mt-4 lg:col-span-7 lg:mt-0">
          <p class="text-base leading-7 text-gray-600">
            YouCongress has been completely <.link
              href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/hectorperezarenas/"
              target="_blank"
              class="underline"
            >self-funded</.link>, except for the $5,000 Future of Life Foundation prize awarded through its Epistemic Case Study Competition. <.link
              href={~p"/future-of-life-foundation-epistack-award"}
              class="underline"
            >Read about the award</.link>.
          </p>
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div id="contribute" class="pt-8 lg:grid lg:grid-cols-12 lg:gap-8">
        <dt class="text-base font-semibold leading-7 text-gray-900 lg:col-span-5">
          How can I contribute?
        </dt>
        <dd class="mt-4 lg:col-span-7 lg:mt-0">
          <p class="text-base   leading-7 text-gray-600">
            You can contribute by adding sourced quotes to statements (policy proposals and claims) or suggesting important missing perspectives. To help review quotes, email us at <.link
              href="mailto:verify@youcongress.org"
              class="underline"
            >
              verify@youcongress.org
            </.link>.
          </p>
          <p class="text-base leading-7 text-gray-600 pt-4">
            If you're a developer, you can contribute on our open source codebase on <.link
              href="https://github.com/youcongress/youcongress"
              target="_blank"
              class="underline"
            >GitHub</.link>. You can also contact us at
            <.link href="mailto:hello@youcongress.org" target="_blank" class="underline">
              hello@youcongress.org
            </.link>
          </p>
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </div>
</div>
